WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.730
GILL WARD: Hello.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.430
Welcome, everybody, to
this afternoon's session.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.320
Thanks for coming.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.260
We're here to talk about driving
product excellence, based

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.320
on feedback from
users like yourselves.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.040
So my name is Gill Ward.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.840
I'm a User Experience
Research lead--

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.266
in G speech-- and a
quantitative UX researcher.

00:00:25.266 --> 00:00:26.640
LINDSAY MOORE:
I'm Lindsay Moore.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.010
I'm a Qualitative User
Experience researcher

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.814
for Google Drive.

00:00:30.814 --> 00:00:31.980
JOSEPH BEARS: I'm Joe Bears.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.990
I'm the Vice President
and Chief Information

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.622
Officer of Hingham
Institution for Savings.

00:00:36.622 --> 00:00:37.830
BEN DRURY: And I'm Ben Drury.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.020
I'm a Qualitative User
Experience researcher,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.092
focused on the Hangouts
video meeting experience.

00:00:43.092 --> 00:00:44.550
So we're going to
talk to you today

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.411
about how we incorporate
user feedback,

00:00:46.411 --> 00:00:48.660
and to develop products that
we think will work really

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:49.854
well for our users.

00:00:49.854 --> 00:00:51.270
We think it's
especially important

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.640
to keep our users'
needs in mind when

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.800
we're developing products for
productivity and collaboration.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.710
So late last year, the
UX director for G Suite,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.099
Lucas Pettinati, published
an article in "Fast Company"

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.550
titled "How to test
products like a Googler."

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.200
In this-- now it's changing.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:10.920
OK.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.420
In this article he
made the argument

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.044
that you should be
collecting feedback,

00:01:14.044 --> 00:01:16.260
not just to make sure what
you're already working on

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:17.718
is the right thing
to be working on

00:01:17.718 --> 00:01:18.990
and to confirm your direction.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.670
Instead, you should
be getting feedback

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.400
in order to figure out new
things you should be working

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.490
on, to uncover new user
needs, and to push your team

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.410
in new and creative directions.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:32.214
So we want to talk a little bit
about how we do this at Google.

00:01:32.214 --> 00:01:33.630
So we're going to
start by sharing

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.879
a little bit of an inside
look at how we focus on user

00:01:35.879 --> 00:01:38.114
journeys, rather than features.

00:01:38.114 --> 00:01:40.530
And then we're going to talk
you through the whole product

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.060
feedback cycle--

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.540
from uncovering user
needs early on, collecting

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.120
early feedback, all the
way through till launch,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.850
when we continue
to collect feedback

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.440
via metrics and analytics.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.535
We're then going to do a deep
dive into how Team Drive ran

00:01:56.535 --> 00:01:59.370
a user feedback program to
get their product to the state

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.190
that it's in today.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.590
So we believe that
in order to build

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.402
robust, excellent
products, you really

00:02:06.402 --> 00:02:07.860
need to ensure that
you're building

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.419
products that are going
to meet your users' needs.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:12.404
So we always have
the end user in mind.

00:02:12.404 --> 00:02:14.070
Before we even start
building something,

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.200
we're talking to users to find
out what problems they have

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.134
and how we can help
solve those problems.

00:02:18.134 --> 00:02:20.550
We want to know how they're
trying to get their work done.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.920
We want the products to
be useful, easy to use,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.366
and always reliable.

00:02:24.366 --> 00:02:25.740
So we've moved
away from thinking

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.750
about individual
features and instead we

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.690
think about the broader goals
that our users have in mind.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.940
I'm going to give you kind
of a silly example of this.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.380
So how many of you
have heard of H-Day.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:37.890
Nobody?

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:38.389
OK.

00:02:38.389 --> 00:02:42.210
So H-Day was a day in
1967 when Sweden switched

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.950
from driving on the
left side of the road

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.830
to driving on the right side of
the road, all overnight, which

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.139
sounds kind of crazy.

00:02:48.139 --> 00:02:49.680
But I want you to
imagine that you're

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.380
living outside of
Stockholm at this time,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.237
just before H-Day happens.

00:02:54.237 --> 00:02:56.070
And you have one goal
in mind every morning,

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.470
and that is to get
to work on time.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.240
So you drive into work and
it's pretty straightforward.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.082
Every day, you follow the same
roads, you make the same turns,

00:03:04.082 --> 00:03:06.540
maybe you're spending more time
admiring the beautiful city

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.498
around you than actually
thinking about how all

00:03:08.498 --> 00:03:10.432
these connections go together.

00:03:10.432 --> 00:03:12.390
But eventually you end
up at this one-way road,

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.332
you turn down it, and,
great, you're at work.

00:03:14.332 --> 00:03:15.540
You've made it there on time.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.700
You've accomplished your goal.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.130
Now H-Day arrives and
everything is switched.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.810
So you're going to be going to
work for the first time driving

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.060
on the other side of the road.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.481
And you're pretty worried about
how this is going to work.

00:03:26.481 --> 00:03:28.230
You leave a little bit
earlier than usual.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.270
Maybe you're driving a
little bit slower than usual.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.040
You might be driving more
carefully than usual.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.570
But, overall, you're
finding that it's actually

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:34.409
working pretty well.

00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:35.950
You're on the other
side of the road,

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.490
but you're still making
good time to work.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:43.350
Until you hit that one-way
road close to your work

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.080
and instead of driving down it,
you see a do not enter sign,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.538
because the one-way
road is flipped

00:03:47.538 --> 00:03:49.409
in the opposite direction.

00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:50.700
So why am I talking about this?

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.440
It represents
exactly why we need

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.179
to focus on journeys
instead of features.

00:03:54.179 --> 00:03:56.220
Because when we look at
each individual component

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.320
of this journey, they
all work the same way

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.290
they always did before.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:01.410
So a two-way road
still allows traffic

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:02.910
to go in two directions.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.236
Signs mean the same thing
that they've always meant.

00:04:05.236 --> 00:04:07.110
And your car fundamentally
works the same way

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.114
that it always worked.

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:10.530
The problem is when you connect
all these pieces together,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.532
you can no longer
accomplish your goal

00:04:12.532 --> 00:04:14.490
of getting to work on
time, because of that one

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:15.642
little tweak at the end.

00:04:15.642 --> 00:04:17.100
So we look at the
overall picture--

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.304
the journey-- and the
user fails at their goal.

00:04:20.304 --> 00:04:22.470
So I want to take this back
to software development.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.217
So we know that our
users don't think

00:04:26.217 --> 00:04:27.300
about individual features.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.070
They just think about what
they want to get done at work,

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.760
and how they can
be most productive.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:34.289
So imagine that you just
want to send a video to one

00:04:34.289 --> 00:04:35.080
of your colleagues.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.430
Your goal is to get this
video to your colleague.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.180
This sounds simple
enough, but it actually

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.300
used to be kind of challenging
in Gmail because of the size

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.640
limits on files that you
could attach to an e-mail.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.310
We've improved this greatly.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.670
And today, when you attach
a large file to an e-mail,

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:54.980
instead of not sending, you are
prompted to upload it to Drive,

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:57.471
and then prompted to share
it with your colleagues.

00:04:57.471 --> 00:04:59.970
This might not be the journey
that you're expecting to take.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.230
But you're able to
achieve your goal.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.813
And it's very easy
for you to do that.

00:05:02.813 --> 00:05:06.197
So we've allowed people to
get their work done quickly.

00:05:06.197 --> 00:05:08.280
So, for some of you, you
might be thinking, great,

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.630
but I don't need to send e-mails
with videos to my colleagues.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.652
That's not like a fundamental
goal of what I do.

00:05:13.652 --> 00:05:14.610
And we understand that.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.526
And that's another really
important point here

00:05:16.526 --> 00:05:18.930
is that people in different
roles have different goals,

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:21.150
and they need to
accomplish different tasks.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.310
Take, for example, your
sales representatives.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.050
They spend a lot of
time on the phone.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.930
Fundamentally, they're all
about setting meetings, getting

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:29.760
feedback from their
customers, and keeping

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.600
track of all of those things--
maybe on a spreadsheet.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.312
Your developers,
on the other hand--

00:05:34.312 --> 00:05:36.270
they're spending time
heads down, writing code,

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.220
trying to build that
next great app that

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.599
can scale in response
to increased traffic.

00:05:40.599 --> 00:05:43.140
Their goal is just to get that
app out the door and make sure

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.947
that it's always up and running.

00:05:44.947 --> 00:05:47.280
And then, finally, your G
Suite admin, which is probably

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.321
some of you in this room.

00:05:48.321 --> 00:05:51.480
You're the buffer between
your end users and Google.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.667
You're there to roll
out new products,

00:05:53.667 --> 00:05:55.500
help people learn how
to use those products.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.570
You might jump on
video to help them

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.090
troubleshoot a new product.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:00.330
And you might jump
into the [? C ?] panel,

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.996
to understand who's
adopting the product

00:06:01.996 --> 00:06:04.140
and where you need to
help people more to start

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:05.496
using the product.

00:06:05.496 --> 00:06:06.870
So the question
now is, how do we

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.285
make sure that we get all
of these voices involved

00:06:09.285 --> 00:06:11.160
as we're getting feedback
from our customers?

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.910
And I'm going to hand it
over to Gill now for that.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:14.820
GILL WARD: Great.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.290
Thanks, Ben.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:17.910
OK.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.850
So let's get into the
heart of this talk--

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:23.670
how we gather user
feedback, and then

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:28.710
how we leverage that to make
an impact in our products.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:32.420
But before we do that, I want to
get a sense of your experience

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:35.970
with user research, and with
providing feedback to Google.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:40.340
So have you ever--
please raise your hand--

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.519
submitted feedback in
product, or through surveys

00:06:43.519 --> 00:06:44.060
and products?

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:44.840
Great.

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:46.400
That's awesome to see.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:51.150
How about participated
in a usability test?

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:51.656
Wow.

00:06:51.656 --> 00:06:53.280
That's a lot more
than I was expecting.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.330
Awesome.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:58.860
Have you been observed by a
researcher in your workplace?

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.030
Even a few of those, too.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:00.810
That's great.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:04.880
So we've had a lot of different
exposure to UX research.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:06.720
And we're going to
talk a little bit more

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.130
about all these
different methods

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:10.440
and how we leverage
them within Google.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:17.880
We're going to break this down
into four different stages--

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:21.510
from an early stage of
discovery and testing

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.640
through trusted
tester, early adopter,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.950
and general availability.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:28.170
And throughout all of
the examples that we're

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:30.840
talking about, we're
going to try and point out

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.220
exactly what impact our research
has had on those products.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.760
Because it's really important to
us that whatever research we do

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:40.050
does have an impact.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.060
We know that your
time is valuable.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.900
And we want to make sure
that we can leverage that.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.360
Saying that, I'm not going
to be able to provide

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.810
pinpointed evidence of all
the impact that we've had,

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:54.930
because some of this research
has just been too recent.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.260
And, obviously, we can't
talk about product direction.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.390
So bear with me there.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:02.760
OK.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:06.230
So let's dive into
the earliest stage.

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:09.290
At this point, we're
defining new products.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:11.450
We're fleshing out
the feature set.

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:13.655
We're pinning down the
details of those products.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.490
There are two main
types of research

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:19.570
that we do at this point.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.360
I'll give you an example of
a discovery type, an example

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:26.090
of a testing type of research.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:36.299
But, before that, who has
heard of eat your own dog food?

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:36.799
OK.

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:37.840
So quite a lot of people.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.100
So the premise behind
this is, before we

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:44.210
give our products to users, we
should be using them ourselves,

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:45.290
right?

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:48.500
And, luckily, we have
a lot of Googlers

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.870
who really care
about our products.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:54.650
And so they will
take time working

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.020
with really early-stage
products that are

00:08:57.020 --> 00:08:59.370
really rough around the edges.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.290
They probably have
lots of bugs in them.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.740
They might not be
quite usable just yet.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.860
But they will give us really
opinionated and critical

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:07.520
feedback.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.930
They do not hold back.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:14.270
And that helps us move
that product forward.

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:18.810
It helps us iron out
those rough edges.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:23.220
And it helps us fix a
lot of those early bugs.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:27.131
But we know that Googlers
aren't like our G Suite users.

00:09:27.131 --> 00:09:27.630
Right?

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.090
In many ways-- one example--

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.970
if a Googler is trying to create
a document or a spreadsheet

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:36.540
or a presentation, typically,
we use our G Suite editors

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:37.816
to do that.

00:09:37.816 --> 00:09:39.690
And we know that, for
a lot of our customers,

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:45.000
on the other hand, Microsoft
is still part and parcel

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.860
of their work day.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.820
So if we just
focused on Googlers,

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:54.270
we wouldn't understand how
our products interfaced

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.100
with Microsoft products.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.860
So that's one example
where we really

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:00.410
need to get outside of Google.

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:04.430
So we do.

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:09.800
We want to, figuratively, peer
in to different workplaces,

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:15.250
to understand what's
happening in our users' lives.

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:17.900
And there were three reasons
why we want to do this.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.070
We want to understand
their user journeys.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:27.030
We want to understand what
they're trying to achieve,

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:28.690
what tools they're using--

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:31.110
whether it's our
tools or other tools--

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.930
what tools they are
using to do that.

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:36.939
The flip side of
that is, of course,

00:10:36.939 --> 00:10:37.980
what's holding them back?

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:40.050
What are the obstacles?

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:42.840
What are the
opportunities that we have

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.250
to improve those user journeys?

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:48.430
And then, thirdly,
we can also get

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:52.570
some really great early
feedback about our new products.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:54.850
If we're thinking-- if
we have a potential idea,

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:56.470
we can test it out
with our users.

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:59.200
Does it resonate with them?

00:10:59.200 --> 00:10:59.940
OK.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:05.880
So the first two of
these we're going

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:09.027
to use this first method,
the discovery type method.

00:11:09.027 --> 00:11:10.860
And that's going to
give us a lot of insight

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.270
into those first two types.

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:22.030
So this discovery,
this exploratory phase,

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:25.370
we're really trying to get
a holistic understanding.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:29.770
We want to see how people
work in the workplace,

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:33.130
relative to their team,
relative to the organization

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:37.240
as a whole, when they're
working by themselves.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:40.000
And this example-- this photo
is taken from an example

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.230
a few years ago.

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:44.410
We took a number of engineers,
product managers, designers,

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:47.920
as well as researchers,
into multiple companies

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:52.790
across the US, and
internationally, too.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:55.400
And we gave them this guidebook.

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:58.770
And, you know, it kind of can
guide them through questions

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:01.380
that they can ask
people that they meet--

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.510
things to look out for.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.370
And they have a
place to write down

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.710
notes, which we then
brought back into Google,

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:10.530
and we could share between us.

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.160
So there were two
really awesome outcomes.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:18.240
The first of these was we got
our engineers, our product

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:22.410
team, talking with
our end users.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:25.830
And that enables
those-- our engineers--

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:28.440
to really gain empathy and
understanding of our users.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.360
It's one of the
most powerful tools

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:34.030
that we have as researchers.

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:35.420
The flip side of
that, of course,

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:38.300
is we have a ton of data.

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:41.260
So we trawled through
all of that data.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.210
And there was so much
information in there--

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:48.160
bugs that needed to be
fixed; feature requests.

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:51.220
But then we also started
to see high level patterns.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.200
And one of those
high level patterns

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.360
was that even three
or four years ago when

00:12:55.360 --> 00:13:00.750
we did this study, mobile was
ubiquitous in the workplace.

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:05.010
And that brought us to
start focusing on mobile,

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:07.830
to ensure that we have parity
between our mobile and desktop

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.000
products.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.700
And we're at the point now
where I can take my phone,

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:14.730
I can start a document;
I can leave comments

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:18.780
on somebody else's documents;
I can collaborate with my team

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:21.460
wherever I am.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:24.370
So we really see
this long-term impact

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:27.759
from this kind of research.

00:13:27.759 --> 00:13:29.050
So that's a discovery research.

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:31.545
On the opposite side,
we have testing.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:41.360
So we do iterative research
on early-stage concepts,

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:43.670
or even later-stage
products, where we just

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:47.010
need to figure out the user
interaction with this product.

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:49.490
We need to iron out those edges.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.820
And we have individuals
either come in to Google

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:57.300
or we interview them
over video chat as well.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:00.800
And we get them to walk
through a scenario using

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:04.370
either the concept
or the product,

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:06.390
and get them to do that
representative task,

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:10.290
so that we can see how
they perform that task.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:13.550
And, you know, there's a
number of different things

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:14.600
we can pull out of that.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:18.530
We get a sense of, does the
concept resonate with them?

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:23.500
Is this a problem that our users
are actually trying to solve?

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:25.180
Does the current set-up--

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:26.770
is it usable?

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:30.460
Can people achieve that goal?

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:32.470
And then we also just get
to hear other concerns

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:35.570
and other feedback.

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:42.220
And so, from this, we
get really early feedback

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.710
that can help us hone the
direction of our product,

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:47.750
and give us
confidence that we are

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:50.960
heading in the right direction.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:56.650
We run these studies
every week, across the US,

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:59.560
across the world.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:01.990
And so, of course, that
means that we always

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:05.170
need more people to test with.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:08.020
So if you would
like to join with us

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:11.500
and provide feedback
in that manner,

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:13.650
we would love to
have you sign up.

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:16.000
There's a couple more QR
codes at the back of the room

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.470
on the way out, too.

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:22.880
This research panel is
for G Suite end users.

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:25.350
We're looking for
companies who will sign up.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:26.850
We can't promise
that everybody will

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:28.994
be able to join the
panel at this stage,

00:15:28.994 --> 00:15:29.910
because it is a pilot.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.550
But we're hoping to get
lots of people signing up,

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:39.150
so that we can continue to
do this early-stage research,

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:46.656
and allow you to have an impact
in our product direction.

00:15:46.656 --> 00:15:49.360
And with that, I'm going to
hand it back over to Ben.

00:15:51.952 --> 00:15:53.410
BEN DRURY: So I'm
going to tell you

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:55.420
a little bit about the
next couple of areas

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:56.910
where we get feedback.

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:59.200
These are trusted testers
and early adopter programs,

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:00.790
which are more
formal collaborations

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:03.310
with small groups of our users.

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:05.560
In both of these phases, we
work with a cross-section

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:06.677
of businesses--

00:16:06.677 --> 00:16:08.260
different sizes,
different verticals--

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:10.468
to try and be as representative
of the kind of people

00:16:10.468 --> 00:16:12.800
that we work with every
day as we possibly can.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:14.552
We're really focused
on fit and finish,

00:16:14.552 --> 00:16:16.510
rather than discovering
new product directions.

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:18.218
But you'll see there
are some times where

00:16:18.218 --> 00:16:20.236
we make some interesting
tweaks to our products.

00:16:20.236 --> 00:16:22.600
I'm going to use the new
Hangouts video meeting

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.480
experience as the example here.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.230
When we kicked off
this project, we really

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:28.360
wanted to take a
fresh new look on how

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:30.910
we could build an
enterprise-focused video

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:35.860
meeting software that would
work well across web, mobile,

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:39.150
and in the in-room experience--
dedicated meeting rooms.

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:42.130
And we wanted to focus on
identifying those core pain

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:44.422
points that people experience
with the offerings today,

00:16:44.422 --> 00:16:46.630
eliminate those, and make
a product that would really

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:49.840
make for effective meetings.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:52.570
So we started with that
early discovery work

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:53.870
that Gill mentioned already.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:56.440
And two really key parts
here were field visits

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:57.240
and interviews.

00:16:57.240 --> 00:16:59.980
So with field visits we
were able to actually go out

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:01.690
to some of our
customers and observe

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:04.329
them using their existing
software to complete

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:05.380
video meetings.

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:07.690
We were able to see these
social interactions, and what

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:09.314
makes a meeting really
flow, and what's

00:17:09.314 --> 00:17:11.970
important to making
a meeting productive.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:13.720
We then did deep-dive
interviews with some

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:15.819
of the people who participated
in those meetings,

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:18.310
to understand how much
those problems come up--

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:21.177
like how frequently do they
encounter those problems?

00:17:21.177 --> 00:17:22.760
And what are the
sort of common themes

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:24.468
that they see, in
terms of trying to make

00:17:24.468 --> 00:17:25.930
their meetings more productive?

00:17:25.930 --> 00:17:28.757
And we learned two really
interesting things here.

00:17:28.757 --> 00:17:31.090
The first is that people
really care about big meetings.

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:32.740
They don't want to have to
worry about the number of people

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:33.640
they're inviting to the meeting.

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:34.300
They just want to
be able to have

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:36.730
as many people in that
meeting as possible.

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:38.620
The second is that
they really care

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:41.539
about people being able to
get into the meeting easily.

00:17:41.539 --> 00:17:42.955
And this doesn't
just mean that we

00:17:42.955 --> 00:17:45.440
should do a great job of getting
people into a video meeting.

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:47.106
It also means that
we have to open it up

00:17:47.106 --> 00:17:49.159
to people who are dialing
in on their phones.

00:17:49.159 --> 00:17:50.950
So to meet market needs,
we need to make it

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:53.080
so that if you have
somebody who's traveling

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:54.490
and doesn't have a
good Wi-Fi connection,

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:56.440
or you have a customer who
just doesn't like showing up

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:59.023
on video conferencing, they're
able to connect to your meeting

00:17:59.023 --> 00:17:59.574
as well.

00:17:59.574 --> 00:18:01.240
So these were two
things that we sort of

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:03.580
incorporated into our work
in building and designing

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:04.307
this new product.

00:18:04.307 --> 00:18:05.890
I'm going to skip
over the phase where

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:08.770
we did some usability
testing and dog-fooding here,

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:11.530
and jump right into our
trusted tester program.

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:14.530
So trusted tester is
an invite-only program,

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:18.550
where UX, product, and sales
works really closely together

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:21.550
to identify a small
subset of our users

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:23.320
who are willing to
take the risk to work

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:27.520
with us with a product that's
not fully fleshed out yet.

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:30.130
We want people who are
responsive, constructively

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:33.181
critical, willing to give
lots of feedback, and also

00:18:33.181 --> 00:18:34.930
willing to experiment
with us a little bit

00:18:34.930 --> 00:18:37.630
as they try out these products.

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:39.184
We ask for a lot of
regular feedback.

00:18:39.184 --> 00:18:41.350
And we're working very,
very closely with our admins

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:42.362
at this point.

00:18:42.362 --> 00:18:44.320
But we think that there's
a mutually beneficial

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:46.240
relationship here,
because the admins also

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.340
get the chance to
see a new product,

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:52.520
and really be involved in
the roadmap for that product.

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:54.490
So we engage closely
with G Suite admins,

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:56.400
trying to build up
their confidence

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.542
that they can roll this product
out to all of their end users.

00:18:59.542 --> 00:19:01.000
The goal is for
them to roll it out

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.583
to everybody in their company,
so that they can then bubble up

00:19:03.583 --> 00:19:05.650
as much feedback as possible
from their end users

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:07.510
to say how well the
product is working

00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:09.252
in supporting user needs.

00:19:09.252 --> 00:19:10.960
So this gives us a
chance to kind of test

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:13.543
the product in the wild, and see
what user journeys people are

00:19:13.543 --> 00:19:15.060
taking with it.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:17.590
In this case, we also
did some observation.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:20.055
So once they had rolled the
product out to their end users,

00:19:20.055 --> 00:19:22.180
we went out and actually
watched them participating

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:24.790
in meetings with the new
Hangouts video meeting

00:19:24.790 --> 00:19:27.040
experience, and then
conducted some surveys

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:30.680
to understand how it compared
to their past experiences.

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:33.280
And, again, we got some
interesting insights here.

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:35.260
Sitting in on those
meetings, we saw

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:38.500
that, at almost every meeting
people have with lots of people

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:43.300
joining on video, somebody
joins with their mic not muted

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:46.771
from a loud room, or from
a bus station or something.

00:19:46.771 --> 00:19:48.020
And this is really disruptive.

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:49.840
You suddenly have somebody
with a lot of background noise

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:50.500
joining a meeting.

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:51.958
So we realized what
we needed to do

00:19:51.958 --> 00:19:54.550
is make it really easy
to quiet that person down

00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:56.890
and try and remove
that disruption.

00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:58.630
So we took the time
to rework the UI

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:01.362
and make it much quicker to
find the controls for muting

00:20:01.362 --> 00:20:02.570
somebody who joins a meeting.

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:05.350
So, as a meeting host,
you have more control

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:06.872
over the meeting experience.

00:20:06.872 --> 00:20:08.580
And we think it's
small tweaks like these

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:10.510
that can really make for
an optimal experience

00:20:10.510 --> 00:20:12.880
for our users.

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:14.950
We love working with
our trusted testers.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.990
Like I said, it's a small group
of admins and their end users.

00:20:17.990 --> 00:20:19.030
They're the kind of
people who we can just

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:21.071
e-mail out of the blue
and they'll respond to us.

00:20:21.071 --> 00:20:22.030
They know who we are.

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:23.190
We work closely.

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:24.790
But what they
can't do is tell us

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:26.957
how the product is
going to work at scale.

00:20:26.957 --> 00:20:28.540
So for that, we need
to ease ourselves

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:32.830
into general availability
and get broader feedback.

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:35.650
So we move into our
early adopter programs.

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:38.950
The core question is, how
does it work at scale?

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:41.710
By the time the product is
in the early adopter program,

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:43.321
it should be pretty stable.

00:20:43.321 --> 00:20:45.820
We know that it's really hard
to deal with change management

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:46.420
as an admin.

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:47.830
So we want our admins
to know that they

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:49.455
can roll this product
out and it's just

00:20:49.455 --> 00:20:52.440
going to work and support all
the use cases of their users.

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:55.225
They should be confident that
it will work for everybody.

00:20:55.225 --> 00:20:57.600
They understand that we're
not going to make big changes.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.100
And we know that if we
did make big changes,

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:00.990
that would be really
difficult on them.

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:02.654
So we announced in
the fall of 2016

00:21:02.654 --> 00:21:04.320
that we are going
into the early adopter

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:08.020
program with the new Hangouts
video meeting experience.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:10.950
And we've really been
focused on fit and finish.

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:13.650
We're now in the process
of collecting end product

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:17.340
feedback, tracking bugs,
and really just seeking

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:19.470
feedback via surveys to
see what more we need

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:21.780
to do to make this even better.

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.337
The next step is going to
be general availability.

00:21:24.337 --> 00:21:25.170
We're not there yet.

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:27.496
But we're not going to stop
collecting feedback when

00:21:27.496 --> 00:21:28.870
we do go to general
availability,

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:29.790
because it's really
important for us

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:32.140
to know how it's working
in the real world.

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:34.740
We'll keep doing-- we'll
keep checking on bugs,

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:36.600
we'll keep tracking
end product feedback.

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:37.974
But, more importantly,
we'll also

00:21:37.974 --> 00:21:39.510
sort of kick off
that cycle again

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:41.884
and start thinking about what
the next product is that we

00:21:41.884 --> 00:21:43.120
might build in this space.

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:45.030
And I'm going to hand
it over to Gill now

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:47.634
just for an example of
how this doesn't always

00:21:47.634 --> 00:21:48.800
work out exactly as planned.

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:52.830
GILL WARD: Thank you, Ben.

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:58.040
So, of course, you couldn't
put up this great--

00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:00.410
create this great process,
but then something

00:22:00.410 --> 00:22:02.870
will come along that doesn't
really fit into that process.

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:05.270
For us, that was Jamboard.

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:06.890
And that's because
Jamboard isn't just

00:22:06.890 --> 00:22:08.640
about great software.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:13.950
It's about testing and producing
great hardware as well.

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:16.400
So, for those of you who
haven't seen Jamboard,

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:19.180
it's Google's take on
the digital white board.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:22.790
We're trying to enable
easier team collaboration.

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:25.154
So if you haven't
touched Jamboard,

00:22:25.154 --> 00:22:27.320
I believe there's one just
out in the corridor here.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:29.540
Go play with it afterwards.

00:22:29.540 --> 00:22:32.660
But you can see, you can draw,
you can write on this Jamboard.

00:22:32.660 --> 00:22:36.140
You can insert images,
virtual stickies,

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:38.990
all sorts of different objects.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:41.914
And you can also access
that Jamboard remotely

00:22:41.914 --> 00:22:42.830
through other devices.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:48.950
So because we're talking
about hardware here,

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:50.990
we had to get really
creative about the process

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:52.160
that we were using.

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:55.820
So instead of going through
trusted tester and then an EAP,

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:58.700
we decided to work really
closely with a few very select

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:00.320
partners.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:05.130
We would go into the
partners' offices.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:08.480
We would observe
people using Jamboard.

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.480
We'd also observe them when they
weren't using Jamboard, right?

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:13.310
When they were doing
brainstorming or other

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:17.630
collaborative work, but they
chose not to include Jamboard.

00:23:17.630 --> 00:23:20.990
We also interviewed
users individually

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:24.530
to get more focused feedback
and understand how Jamboard

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:27.120
fit into their user journeys.

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:29.690
And we also
interviewed IT admins.

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:31.920
Because, again,
like Ben was saying,

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:34.250
we understand that the
IT admin is that conduit.

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:39.170
IT admins are
going to be the key

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:42.030
to the success of
deploying and managing

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:44.120
Jamboards out in the wild.

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:47.660
All right.

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:51.780
So we've talked about
early-stage research.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:53.510
We've talked about
trusted tester--

00:23:53.510 --> 00:23:55.160
how that's like the
first time that it

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:56.840
sees the customer on scale.

00:23:56.840 --> 00:23:59.970
But it still might
change a little bit.

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:04.340
We've seen early
access programs, where

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:07.360
the product is more finalized.

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:11.360
And, of course, once we launch,
we don't just stop there.

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:11.860
Right?

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:15.310
Our methods of research
of gathering user feedback

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:16.900
just change.

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:18.950
We now have bigger scale.

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:21.370
So we use end product feedback.

00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:26.450
We use end product
surveys, as well.

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:28.410
That information is
really important to us.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:32.240
We have teams that will
process that feedback, that

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:35.840
create groupings that
categorize that feedback,

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:39.620
so that we can understand
both whether there

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:42.156
are new problems
arising, whether we fixed

00:24:42.156 --> 00:24:44.780
the problems that we were trying
to fix, and just to understand

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:47.840
what's going on with our users.

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:51.740
And we can also look at
behavior-- actual behavior

00:24:51.740 --> 00:24:52.370
in the product.

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:53.369
What are people doing?

00:24:53.369 --> 00:24:54.910
What features are
they engaging with?

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:57.410
And triangulating all
these different sources

00:24:57.410 --> 00:25:02.190
of data or feedback can help
us then prioritize the product

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:05.410
roadmap moving ahead.

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:06.820
And I do want to point out--

00:25:06.820 --> 00:25:11.530
one of the surveys we do send
out, regularly, every quarter,

00:25:11.530 --> 00:25:13.840
is to IT admins and developers.

00:25:13.840 --> 00:25:16.750
Because, as we've said
multiple times already

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:18.880
through this
presentation, we know

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:22.060
that you have very
specific user journeys;

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:27.250
that it's critical for us
that we understand your pain

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:30.400
points and your needs,
so that we can ensure

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:34.430
that our products get rolled out
successfully to the end users.

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:36.490
So if you do see
this in your inbox,

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:39.760
please consider taking
time to fill it out.

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:45.400
So we've talked about the
four different stages.

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:48.370
I want to mention, too, being
a qualitative researcher,

00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:51.460
that we also take into
account quantitative feedback

00:25:51.460 --> 00:25:53.530
through all four
of these stages.

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:57.280
And by quantitative
feedback, I mean metrics.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:01.780
And one way that
we use metrics is

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:05.950
by pulling together that
quantitative information

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:09.190
with the qualitative feedback
that we've got from our users

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:11.890
to make sure that we're ready
to move from one of these stages

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:13.420
to the next stage.

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:17.150
Are we ready to go from
trusted tester to EAP?

00:26:17.150 --> 00:26:20.370
Are we ready to go
to general access?

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:23.310
And we want to think beyond just
click-through rates and page

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:25.530
views at this point, too.

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:27.930
And so one of the ways
that we do that at Google

00:26:27.930 --> 00:26:32.100
is by using this framework
with a very cute acronym.

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:34.830
It's talking about the
range of different metrics

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:38.700
that we should be thinking about
when we're considering user--

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:41.730
or considering measuring
how our users are

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:44.430
interacting with our product.

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:45.990
There are five areas here.

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:49.380
I'm going to focus on just two.

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:50.940
And these two are
the ones that I've

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:58.360
seen come up again and again
as we push new products out.

00:26:58.360 --> 00:27:02.890
So retention-- retention is
all about long-term engagement.

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:05.990
Does a person keep coming back?

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:09.530
And, for different products,
we expect different levels

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:11.330
of engagement over time, right?

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:13.110
For Gmail-- if you're
anything like me--

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:15.560
I'm in there
multiple times a day.

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:19.100
For presentations,
not quite as often.

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:20.300
I'm not senior enough yet.

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:22.550
I do a lot of other work, too.

00:27:22.550 --> 00:27:25.670
So it's not about how often
people are coming back,

00:27:25.670 --> 00:27:29.150
but that that number stays
pretty stable over time.

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:32.372
We're keeping people coming
back at a certain rate.

00:27:32.372 --> 00:27:33.830
So for Gmail it
might be every day.

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:36.550
For presentations, every
week or every month.

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:41.880
And the Gmail example's
a little forced here,

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:45.480
because people-- we
know that our end users,

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:46.699
they have to do e-mail.

00:27:46.699 --> 00:27:48.240
They have no choice
about the matter.

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:49.650
They have to be
in Gmail checking

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:54.160
their e-mail, for the majority
of our G Suite customers.

00:27:54.160 --> 00:27:58.030
And so that's why we also
want to look at happiness.

00:27:58.030 --> 00:28:03.930
So happiness is
about user opinions.

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:07.560
It's how happy is a user.

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:10.890
Often, we use satisfaction
as a measure of this.

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:13.140
You'll probably see that
in end product surveys--

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:16.350
how satisfied are you with this
product on a scale of 1 to 5,

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:21.510
from very happy or very
satisfied to very unsatisfied?

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:25.580
And we track those metrics over
time for our existing products.

00:28:25.580 --> 00:28:28.950
We want to make sure that
they're steadily increasing.

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:30.960
And we also set
thresholds for those--

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:32.780
for new products
and new features

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:33.740
that we want to launch.

00:28:36.970 --> 00:28:41.320
So HEART can tell us about
the breadth of metrics

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:43.990
that we're interested in using.

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:48.730
One principle that overrides
all of these five areas

00:28:48.730 --> 00:28:51.580
is that our metrics should
be aligned with the goals

00:28:51.580 --> 00:28:55.100
that we have for our user.

00:28:55.100 --> 00:29:01.040
And by that I mean, how are we
trying to help our user achieve

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:01.910
their user journey?

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:08.750
And we also want to take
into account who that user is

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:11.200
as part of that, too.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.440
So to illustrate
that in an example,

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:15.690
let's talk about Google Sites.

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:20.250
So we released a new version
of Google Sites late last year.

00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:24.420
And one of the user goals,
as part of this release,

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:27.810
was that it was easier
for people to create sites

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:30.690
to share information.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:33.960
And so, instead of just looking
at how often did somebody click

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:37.670
on a page that was a new
site versus an old site,

00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:41.670
we tried to align it with
this idea of creating sites.

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:44.010
So one of the
metrics we looked at

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:48.870
was we asked people
who'd created the site--

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:51.510
so that was our user type--

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:53.760
how easy it was to do that.

00:29:56.860 --> 00:29:59.400
And then we compared that
with how easy people reported

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:04.005
it was to create sites through
the old version of the product.

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:09.920
And another metric that was
associated with that user goal

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:11.450
was around retention.

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:15.560
The next time that person
had to create another site,

00:30:15.560 --> 00:30:17.290
did they do so with
the new product,

00:30:17.290 --> 00:30:20.340
or did they choose to go
back to the old product?

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:23.330
So we had that happiness
and retention metric.

00:30:23.330 --> 00:30:25.730
And with those two metrics--

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:28.220
along with user feedback--

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:30.440
we felt we could
safely make a decision

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:31.460
to launch that product.

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:38.990
All right.

00:30:38.990 --> 00:30:43.840
So, just to recap, we
have these four stages--

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:49.010
the early stage
testing and discovery;

00:30:49.010 --> 00:30:52.070
releasing to our
trusted testers,

00:30:52.070 --> 00:30:54.860
where things might
change a little bit;

00:30:54.860 --> 00:30:57.350
then early adopter
program, where

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:00.775
we're releasing it more
widely, with the assumption

00:31:00.775 --> 00:31:02.900
that things aren't going
to change too much, right?

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:05.780
We're just doing a little
polishing, a little fixing,

00:31:05.780 --> 00:31:07.970
before we launch;
and then, finally,

00:31:07.970 --> 00:31:11.540
launching to general
availability.

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:15.320
And, at that, I'd like to
invite Lindsay to come and talk

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:16.710
about Team Drive.

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:27.040
LINDSAY MOORE: So, to start,
can I get a show of hands--

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:31.640
has anyone here not
heard of Team Drives?

00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:32.140
A couple.

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:37.350
So to catch you up if you're
not familiar with it--

00:31:37.350 --> 00:31:39.550
Team Drives are new
collaborative spaces

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:40.720
in Google Drive.

00:31:40.720 --> 00:31:43.240
And they live
parallel to My Drive,

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:47.170
but they're specifically
created with teams in mind.

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:49.300
And they're designed
around three core tenets.

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:53.890
So first-- files stay after
employees leave the company.

00:31:53.890 --> 00:31:56.620
Second, the sharing rules are
much simpler than the sharing

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:58.780
rules in Drive today.

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:00.790
And then, lastly, they
get your team's content

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:02.080
all in one place.

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:03.940
So with Team Drives,
shared spaces

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:06.160
now look the same for everyone.

00:32:06.160 --> 00:32:08.410
New members joining
a team automatically

00:32:08.410 --> 00:32:11.170
get access to the content
they need right away.

00:32:11.170 --> 00:32:14.990
And files don't go
missing accidentally.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:17.780
So with Team Drives,
we went through each

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:21.920
of those phases of getting
user feedback that Ben

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:24.020
and Gill described as
we were progressing

00:32:24.020 --> 00:32:26.240
towards general availability.

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:28.640
And through those phases,
we had three main goals.

00:32:28.640 --> 00:32:30.860
So we needed to determine
whether Team Drives,

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:33.770
as a feature, was
fit for launch.

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:36.170
And we also needed to
understand what works well

00:32:36.170 --> 00:32:37.730
or, maybe, what
doesn't work well,

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:39.410
when customers start
to use Team Drives

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:42.410
for their actual
day-to-day collaboration.

00:32:42.410 --> 00:32:43.940
And then, lastly,
we really wanted

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:47.270
to gather longitudinal
feedback about how

00:32:47.270 --> 00:32:50.870
domains and teams and
users within those domains

00:32:50.870 --> 00:32:53.660
incorporate Team Drives
into their workflow.

00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:55.550
And then, how would
they use it differently

00:32:55.550 --> 00:32:58.660
from shared folders?

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:00.520
So can I get in
their show of hands--

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:02.490
how many of you are
from domains that

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:04.860
participated in our
early adopter program

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:05.830
for Team Drives?

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:07.180
Anybody?

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:07.680
Cool.

00:33:07.680 --> 00:33:09.011
That's awesome.

00:33:09.011 --> 00:33:09.510
OK.

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:10.980
So Early Adopter Program--

00:33:10.980 --> 00:33:13.980
or EAP-- it was our
first, large-scale test

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:15.210
of Team Drives.

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:18.090
And, at first, was with
around 100 domains, and later,

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:19.590
thousands of domains.

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:21.660
And we were gathering
both qualitative

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:23.850
and quantitative
data that we could

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:26.460
use to evaluate our
success metrics,

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:28.590
and gauge our launch readiness.

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:31.740
And it was significant because
it represented the first time

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:34.920
a large amount of G Suite
admins could start testing out

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:37.530
Team Drives, and thinking
about how to deploy it

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:39.660
within their domains.

00:33:39.660 --> 00:33:42.180
And then, also, it was
significant in that we had

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:44.430
to sign up customers at scale.

00:33:44.430 --> 00:33:46.500
We were getting
feedback at scale.

00:33:46.500 --> 00:33:49.350
We were responding to
that feedback at scale.

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:51.690
And that took an immense
amount of coordination

00:33:51.690 --> 00:33:55.590
across many different
groups at Google.

00:33:55.590 --> 00:33:58.850
So, like I said, we initially
started out our early adopter

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:02.030
program with just
under 100 domains.

00:34:02.030 --> 00:34:04.970
And it was an internationally
representative group

00:34:04.970 --> 00:34:06.510
of customers--

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:09.710
a range of sizes,
range of industries,

00:34:09.710 --> 00:34:11.630
all different
types of companies.

00:34:11.630 --> 00:34:14.420
And, as a requirement
for getting access

00:34:14.420 --> 00:34:18.050
during this early part of
our early adopter program,

00:34:18.050 --> 00:34:22.250
customers had to be willing
to give access to Team Drives

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:26.210
to at least some of
their non-IT users,

00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:29.389
and also to have those users
participate in some feedback

00:34:29.389 --> 00:34:30.800
surveys.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:34.969
So we knew that when we
gave access to Team Drives,

00:34:34.969 --> 00:34:38.840
we could rely on G
Suite admins to give us

00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:41.239
their feedback as they started
trying it out and thinking

00:34:41.239 --> 00:34:44.750
about how they were
planning on deploying it.

00:34:44.750 --> 00:34:48.920
But we also really needed to
balance that with feedback

00:34:48.920 --> 00:34:50.460
from end users, as well.

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:53.929
So, for example, we wanted to
be able to measure whether we're

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:57.050
hitting our success metrics
around adoption and engagement.

00:34:57.050 --> 00:34:59.970
So, were users actually
trying Team Drives?

00:34:59.970 --> 00:35:01.700
Did they come back to
it and try it again

00:35:01.700 --> 00:35:04.190
after they tried
it that first time?

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:07.580
And we needed to understand,
was it actually addressing

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:10.730
the user and team
and domain level

00:35:10.730 --> 00:35:12.570
needs that it was
intended to address?

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:14.970
And was it generating
satisfaction?

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:18.350
So, because of this,
we ran a survey

00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:20.960
with both admins and users.

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:23.900
And we did that before anyone
had access to Team Drives.

00:35:23.900 --> 00:35:25.700
And that became our
baseline for things

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:27.740
like satisfaction, happiness.

00:35:27.740 --> 00:35:30.054
And then we ran another
survey after people had tried

00:35:30.054 --> 00:35:31.220
Team Drives for a few weeks.

00:35:31.220 --> 00:35:34.210
So that way we were able
to get a good baseline

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:37.220
and then use that
to evaluate success.

00:35:37.220 --> 00:35:38.930
And through those
surveys we were also

00:35:38.930 --> 00:35:40.760
able to get an
understanding of the whys

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:42.470
behind the usage metrics we saw.

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:44.630
So why were people
using Team Drives,

00:35:44.630 --> 00:35:46.440
or not using Team Drives.

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:48.830
So thank you so much
for those of you who

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:53.182
participated in this early phase
of our early adopter program,

00:35:53.182 --> 00:35:55.640
and participated in surveys,
and had your users participate

00:35:55.640 --> 00:35:56.140
in surveys.

00:35:56.140 --> 00:36:00.560
It's just such a valuable
source of feedback for us.

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:02.720
So what did we learn
from our early adopter

00:36:02.720 --> 00:36:04.070
program for Team Drives?

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:07.790
Well, in addition to a
host of useful feedback,

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:10.520
we had two big takeaways.

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:13.040
And one of those was
around change management.

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:16.500
So we often talk about change
management at the domain level.

00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:19.160
So, as we work
with G Suite admins

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:21.980
to understand what's needed
for domains to change

00:36:21.980 --> 00:36:24.200
their processes, or
deploy new products,

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:26.770
features, and educate users.

00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:29.960
But in our EAP, we heard
that teams themselves

00:36:29.960 --> 00:36:31.472
need change management.

00:36:31.472 --> 00:36:32.930
So with a feature
like Team Drives,

00:36:32.930 --> 00:36:34.100
it's designed for teams.

00:36:34.100 --> 00:36:37.280
And the whole team needs
to be coordinated and have

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:40.270
critical mass to actually make
a switch of that magnitude--

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:42.530
a switch in their processes
around where they're

00:36:42.530 --> 00:36:44.490
storing their team's content.

00:36:44.490 --> 00:36:46.460
So we've worked to
incorporate onboarding

00:36:46.460 --> 00:36:49.880
into the feature that
helps teams understand

00:36:49.880 --> 00:36:53.980
Team Drives as they get
started with using it.

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:56.140
The other was a
confirmation that being

00:36:56.140 --> 00:36:58.060
able to migrate
your team's existing

00:36:58.060 --> 00:37:01.060
content into Team Drives is
really crucial for adoption.

00:37:01.060 --> 00:37:03.820
So today teams have their
Google Drive content

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:05.930
in large, shared
folder structures,

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:07.990
with a lot of
different file owners.

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:10.870
And both G Suite
admins and users

00:37:10.870 --> 00:37:14.820
need the ability to easily
move files they don't own,

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:19.480
and folders full of files they
don't own, into a Team Drive.

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:23.050
For admins, often it's
this initial, bulk content

00:37:23.050 --> 00:37:24.730
migration, trying
to get everything

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:26.680
into Team Drives
for the first time.

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:29.950
But, also, in larger
domains, those admins

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:32.650
need certain users, or
maybe even all their users,

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:34.960
to help them with the
scale of that task.

00:37:34.960 --> 00:37:37.222
And then, in a lot of
companies' cultures,

00:37:37.222 --> 00:37:38.680
you know, the teams
themselves need

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:41.470
to be able to move their
existing folders of content

00:37:41.470 --> 00:37:43.690
into Team Drives as needed,
without having to involve

00:37:43.690 --> 00:37:46.370
their G Suite admins.

00:37:46.370 --> 00:37:48.860
So we were working to
address these findings.

00:37:48.860 --> 00:37:52.430
And we reached out to customers
who had previously contacted us

00:37:52.430 --> 00:37:55.744
with feedback about that very
thing, about content migration.

00:37:55.744 --> 00:37:58.160
We needed to make sure we were
going to get the experience

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:00.950
right for admins and users
across different kinds

00:38:00.950 --> 00:38:04.160
of domains, different
expectations around access

00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:06.930
or sharing or the
need for control.

00:38:06.930 --> 00:38:09.890
So, really, the ability to
reach back out to customers

00:38:09.890 --> 00:38:12.560
like this is an important
part of what makes

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:15.290
trusted tester and early
adopter program so powerful.

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:18.020
We're really trying to create
a relationship-- cultivate

00:38:18.020 --> 00:38:20.630
that relationship--
where we are able to have

00:38:20.630 --> 00:38:23.150
a back-and-forth conversation
about our products

00:38:23.150 --> 00:38:25.430
and features.

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:27.470
So one of this customers
that we reached out to

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:30.860
was Hingham Institution for
Savings, which is a community

00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:32.600
bank in the Boston area.

00:38:32.600 --> 00:38:35.300
So I will now bring
Joe Bears, who

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:36.764
is the CIO at
Hingham up on stage,

00:38:36.764 --> 00:38:38.180
and we'll hear
from him about what

00:38:38.180 --> 00:38:40.514
it was like to participate
in giving feedback to Google.

00:38:40.514 --> 00:38:41.638
JOSEPH BEARS: Hi, everyone.

00:38:41.638 --> 00:38:43.450
LINDSAY MOORE:
[INAUDIBLE] great.

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:47.840
So we understand that you guys
are trying to transition more

00:38:47.840 --> 00:38:49.280
fully into the cloud.

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:51.770
And moving to G Suite
is a part of that.

00:38:51.770 --> 00:38:54.380
So tell us why that's
important for Hingham.

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:56.360
JOSEPH BEARS: For
multiple reasons.

00:38:56.360 --> 00:39:00.370
Server consolidation being one
of them, and disaster recovery.

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:02.210
With G Suite, we've
been able to get out

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:03.950
of the business of
maintaining servers.

00:39:03.950 --> 00:39:06.580
We've cut down about a
third of our servers.

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:08.900
And we're not doing
things like backup on them

00:39:08.900 --> 00:39:13.120
anymore, doing anti-virus
updates, things of that nature.

00:39:13.120 --> 00:39:14.990
Instead, we're able
to take more time,

00:39:14.990 --> 00:39:16.620
you know, advancing
the company goals,

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:19.490
doing things that
help our customers.

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:20.840
LINDSAY MOORE: So why Drive?

00:39:20.840 --> 00:39:22.982
How is Drive an
important piece of that?

00:39:22.982 --> 00:39:24.440
JOSEPH BEARS:
Drive's brought a lot

00:39:24.440 --> 00:39:26.540
of sharing and collaboration.

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:29.720
Previously, with our old
productivity suite, we were--

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:30.671
somebody would go in.

00:39:30.671 --> 00:39:33.170
We'd have a group of people
that needed to update something.

00:39:33.170 --> 00:39:35.830
And they'd go in,
try to update it,

00:39:35.830 --> 00:39:38.210
and they'd tell them that
it was a read-only copy.

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:39.770
That would result
in users ending up

00:39:39.770 --> 00:39:41.010
having to make a copy.

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:41.930
And then, we'd all--

00:39:41.930 --> 00:39:44.055
somebody would have to come
along and roll back all

00:39:44.055 --> 00:39:46.010
those changes into one copy.

00:39:46.010 --> 00:39:48.350
With Google Now, we're able to--

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:50.070
everybody can work
on it the same time.

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:51.740
So there's a lot
more collaboration.

00:39:51.740 --> 00:39:55.320
And it saves a lot
of efficiencies.

00:39:55.320 --> 00:39:57.630
LINDSAY MOORE: So
why did you guys

00:39:57.630 --> 00:39:59.550
want to be part of
the early adopter

00:39:59.550 --> 00:40:01.350
program for Team Drives?

00:40:01.350 --> 00:40:05.050
JOSEPH BEARS: Initially,
when we set up the--

00:40:05.050 --> 00:40:07.230
when we got into
Drive, we needed

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:10.670
to have a common area just
to share all the data.

00:40:10.670 --> 00:40:12.210
And what we did
there was we created

00:40:12.210 --> 00:40:13.710
what I call a service account.

00:40:13.710 --> 00:40:16.980
And with that service account we
replicated our folder structure

00:40:16.980 --> 00:40:17.480
into it.

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:20.082
And then, from there,
we shared all that out.

00:40:20.082 --> 00:40:21.540
When we saw Team
Drives come along,

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:23.581
we knew that it looked
like what we were actually

00:40:23.581 --> 00:40:24.690
trying to accomplish.

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:25.523
LINDSAY MOORE: Yeah.

00:40:25.523 --> 00:40:28.380
So it sounds like that that
wasn't optimal for management.

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:30.280
And that Team Drives was more
like what you were looking for.

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:31.905
JOSEPH BEARS: Yeah,
it was exactly what

00:40:31.905 --> 00:40:32.800
we were looking for.

00:40:32.800 --> 00:40:35.040
LINDSAY MOORE: So what
did you do when you first

00:40:35.040 --> 00:40:36.232
got access to Team Drives?

00:40:36.232 --> 00:40:37.940
JOSEPH BEARS: We
started by setting it up

00:40:37.940 --> 00:40:39.540
in one of our back offices.

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:42.600
And we ran with that for
approximately a month, two

00:40:42.600 --> 00:40:43.720
months.

00:40:43.720 --> 00:40:46.280
And, from there, we started
spreading out and moving it

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:47.460
to different departments.

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:51.100
It really rolled out quite easy.

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:54.170
From there, we even had some
of our branches even set

00:40:54.170 --> 00:40:56.900
up their own Team Drives and
migrated the data on their own.

00:40:56.900 --> 00:40:58.890
So it worked really well.

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:00.800
LINDSAY MOORE: So
speaking of users,

00:41:00.800 --> 00:41:02.750
how did you introduce
Team Drives to them?

00:41:02.750 --> 00:41:06.260
Like how did you orient people
to just a totally new file

00:41:06.260 --> 00:41:08.365
sharing system?

00:41:08.365 --> 00:41:10.490
JOSEPH BEARS: It's been a
pretty smooth transition.

00:41:10.490 --> 00:41:14.450
We started by replicating the
whole folder structure that

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:15.800
was on our old servers.

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:18.790
And from there, we--

00:41:18.790 --> 00:41:19.290
sorry.

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:22.624
Lost my-- you know, from there,
we rolled it out to everybody.

00:41:22.624 --> 00:41:24.290
And working through
a web browser really

00:41:24.290 --> 00:41:26.248
didn't turn out to be
that much of a difference

00:41:26.248 --> 00:41:27.680
in working through
File Explorer.

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:29.775
So it really wasn't
a huge change.

00:41:29.775 --> 00:41:30.650
LINDSAY MOORE: Great.

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:33.754
So how have people have been
using Team Drive so far?

00:41:33.754 --> 00:41:35.420
JOSEPH BEARS: The
best way to explain it

00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:37.040
is sharing and collaboration.

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:39.590
Like I said, everybody's able
to do work a lot smoother

00:41:39.590 --> 00:41:41.150
and a lot more efficiently.

00:41:41.150 --> 00:41:43.992
So it's really worked
out well in that sense.

00:41:43.992 --> 00:41:45.950
LINDSAY MOORE: And do
you have any specific use

00:41:45.950 --> 00:41:49.010
cases for a team at Hingham
that you can share with us?

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:51.260
JOSEPH BEARS: Probably our
human resources department,

00:41:51.260 --> 00:41:53.490
the onboarding process.

00:41:53.490 --> 00:41:56.240
It used to be a process where
the human resources would

00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:59.330
send e-mails around, and then
attach like the onboarding

00:41:59.330 --> 00:42:00.134
form.

00:42:00.134 --> 00:42:01.550
And now, with Team
Drives, they're

00:42:01.550 --> 00:42:04.010
just setting it up within
the Team Drive folders.

00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:07.990
And then they send out a
notification via Team Drives

00:42:07.990 --> 00:42:10.910
that everybody just goes in and
works on it as they need be.

00:42:10.910 --> 00:42:14.210
And it's a process that
was getting held up before.

00:42:14.210 --> 00:42:16.080
And it's no longer
getting held up.

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:16.580
It's great.

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:18.163
LINDSAY MOORE: That's
so good to hear.

00:42:18.163 --> 00:42:20.780
So, like I mentioned
earlier, Hingham

00:42:20.780 --> 00:42:23.120
had ended up participating
in this follow-up

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:26.810
study we did, at some point
during our early adopter

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:28.760
program, about
content migration.

00:42:28.760 --> 00:42:31.010
So why did you guys want to
participate again in that?

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:32.843
JOSEPH BEARS: Well, we
wanted to have input.

00:42:32.843 --> 00:42:34.550
Like I said, we had that user--

00:42:34.550 --> 00:42:38.899
the utility user, as I call it--
set up, that we have data in.

00:42:38.899 --> 00:42:41.190
And we wanted to have input
on how to get that data out

00:42:41.190 --> 00:42:42.830
of the utility user
and move it over

00:42:42.830 --> 00:42:44.900
into the Team Drive folders.

00:42:44.900 --> 00:42:46.920
Additionally, we're
changing as a company.

00:42:46.920 --> 00:42:48.770
And we want to have
more partners that

00:42:48.770 --> 00:42:50.941
are open to feedback.

00:42:50.941 --> 00:42:52.940
We have other partners
that we give feedback to,

00:42:52.940 --> 00:42:54.590
and they're not
great at responding.

00:42:54.590 --> 00:42:56.780
To contrast that, we
actually have one partner

00:42:56.780 --> 00:43:00.310
that's involved in doing
a licensing audit with us,

00:43:00.310 --> 00:43:03.580
and it's not really helping
to advance the company's goal.

00:43:03.580 --> 00:43:05.210
It's more like a sales tactic.

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:07.570
So it's great to have Google.

00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:09.770
And they're listening
to our feedback.

00:43:09.770 --> 00:43:13.460
And we see it show up very
quickly in the products.

00:43:13.460 --> 00:43:14.960
LINDSAY MOORE: Great.

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:17.300
For your users
themselves, what do they

00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:19.880
get out of participating
in something like this?

00:43:19.880 --> 00:43:22.610
JOSEPH BEARS: A lot of our
users actually commented to me

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:24.350
that they enjoyed doing the--

00:43:24.350 --> 00:43:26.850
they enjoyed the whole
process of giving feedback.

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:28.850
And some of them actually
said they learned more

00:43:28.850 --> 00:43:30.320
about the product
in the process.

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:32.105
So that was a
really great thing.

00:43:32.105 --> 00:43:33.320
LINDSAY MOORE: Cool.

00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:36.470
So when you participate in
an early adopter program,

00:43:36.470 --> 00:43:40.010
like you did, it's not a
finished product, right?

00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:42.270
So how does that impact you?

00:43:42.270 --> 00:43:45.050
Like, what are your
thoughts on using products

00:43:45.050 --> 00:43:46.434
that aren't fully finished?

00:43:46.434 --> 00:43:47.600
JOSEPH BEARS: We don't mind.

00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:50.177
We realize that that's
something that's

00:43:50.177 --> 00:43:52.010
going to occur with a
new product like that.

00:43:52.010 --> 00:43:53.780
And we've made a
decision as a company

00:43:53.780 --> 00:43:56.270
that we want to be more on
the leading edge of technology

00:43:56.270 --> 00:43:58.760
like that, instead of the
trailing edge, where stuff

00:43:58.760 --> 00:43:59.960
is more fully fleshed out.

00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:04.190
But, you know, it
works great for us.

00:44:04.190 --> 00:44:05.990
LINDSAY MOORE: So
just to wrap up--

00:44:05.990 --> 00:44:08.370
what does it mean for an
organization like Hingham

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:10.982
to be involved in this
feedback process with Google?

00:44:10.982 --> 00:44:12.440
JOSEPH BEARS: It
means a lot to us.

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:14.780
It's great that a
small company like ours

00:44:14.780 --> 00:44:19.370
can have feedback into a
product that Google's creating.

00:44:19.370 --> 00:44:22.130
We have another data center
that we're constantly

00:44:22.130 --> 00:44:26.230
trying to give feedback to,
and they don't really listen.

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:29.210
They ask for the
feedback and we never

00:44:29.210 --> 00:44:30.470
see it get into the product.

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:32.690
So it's great to
see it, you know,

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:36.557
see the changes that we ask
for end up in a product.

00:44:36.557 --> 00:44:38.390
LINDSAY MOORE: Well,
thank you so much, Joe,

00:44:38.390 --> 00:44:42.549
for coming here and speaking
with us today, and sharing

00:44:42.549 --> 00:44:44.840
Hingham's experience with
the Team Drives early adopter

00:44:44.840 --> 00:44:45.339
program.

00:44:45.339 --> 00:44:46.420
We really appreciate it.

00:44:46.420 --> 00:44:48.470
And then, for those
of you in the audience

00:44:48.470 --> 00:44:50.930
who were part of that
early adopter program

00:44:50.930 --> 00:44:53.510
as well-- thank you so
much for being part of it,

00:44:53.510 --> 00:44:57.080
taking the time to share
your feedback with us.

00:44:57.080 --> 00:44:58.840
JOSEPH BEARS: Thank you.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:00.280
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:04.054 --> 00:45:06.220
LINDSAY MOORE: So what does
it mean to test products

00:45:06.220 --> 00:45:07.450
like a Googler?

00:45:07.450 --> 00:45:10.540
It means we are always looking
for feedback on our products,

00:45:10.540 --> 00:45:13.360
no matter what stage of the
development cycle we're in.

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:16.570
And we're always looking for
new ways to engage our customers

00:45:16.570 --> 00:45:19.180
and pull in their
valued feedback.

00:45:19.180 --> 00:45:21.250
We're really just
in constant motion

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:24.110
through this research cycle
with each of our products.

00:45:24.110 --> 00:45:27.230
And in order for us to get
this valuable user feedback,

00:45:27.230 --> 00:45:29.570
it's really vital that we
have partners to work with.

00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:32.620
So without their help-- which
is, essentially, your help--

00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:35.120
this would be much harder.

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:37.700
So if you are interested,
like Gill said earlier,

00:45:37.700 --> 00:45:40.970
in having your company join
our G Suite user panel,

00:45:40.970 --> 00:45:44.730
so we can include your end users
in our early iterative studies,

00:45:44.730 --> 00:45:46.190
for example--

00:45:46.190 --> 00:45:48.560
follow this link up
here on the left.

00:45:48.560 --> 00:45:50.990
So you can sign up
and express interest.

00:45:50.990 --> 00:45:53.330
And then, for any of
you in the audience who

00:45:53.330 --> 00:45:55.130
are IT professionals
or developers,

00:45:55.130 --> 00:45:57.080
you can also sign
up using the link

00:45:57.080 --> 00:46:00.180
on the right to help product
development on Google Cloud

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:01.100
Platform.

00:46:01.100 --> 00:46:04.250
So, in either case, it's like
a quick little five-minute

00:46:04.250 --> 00:46:04.790
survey.

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:06.560
You'll get your name
in our database.

00:46:06.560 --> 00:46:09.020
And we can't promise we'll
be able to include everyone

00:46:09.020 --> 00:46:09.800
in our panels.

00:46:09.800 --> 00:46:11.420
But we're really
continually looking

00:46:11.420 --> 00:46:13.544
for more participants
for our studies.

00:46:13.544 --> 00:46:15.710
And we really look forward
to getting your feedback,

00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:18.380
so we can continue to drive
product excellence within G

00:46:18.380 --> 00:46:19.730
Suite.

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:21.230
Thank you so much.

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:23.030
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:28.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

